"The prognosis is driven by several significant factors, including a preoperative PSA of 16.8 and an ISUP grade group 5 Gleason pattern on the prostatectomy specimen. Further adverse findings include pathological T4 stage, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and lymphovascular invasion. These elements suggest high-risk disease.\n\nCountervailing or mitigating information is limited; specifically, there was no documented lymph node metastasis in the surgical report. The biopsy source provided Gleason pattern data, but the pathology report notes that the patient underwent only one biopsy session, and the ISUP grade from this biopsy is missing, widening uncertainty regarding potential discrepancies between biopsy and prostatectomy findings.\n\nFuture prognosis assessment would be significantly revised by obtaining postoperative PSA levels and tracking their subsequent trajectory over time. The absence of documented follow-up data limits current prognostic certainty. The fixed estimated horizon is 34.46 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 25.85–43.08 months; its coverage has not been validated."
